---
// Settings — the account's own preferences, reachable from the account menu on
// every screen in the app.
//
// THE FIVE QUESTIONS (product brief §5):
//
// 1. THE ONE QUESTION THIS SCREEN ANSWERS: "how do I want this app set up for
//    me?" Everything on it changes what one person sees or receives, and nothing
//    on it changes a record. That boundary is what makes it one screen instead
//    of two — the tenant's configuration (users, organizations, funding sources,
//    custom pages) lives under Manage in the nav and is a different screen for a
//    different reader with a different blast radius.
//
// 2. WHO LANDS HERE, AND WHO IS HARDEST. All four readers eventually, but the
//    hard one is the sponsor/reporter who arrives once, having been told the app
//    is hard to read or that they are getting too much mail. They have no model
//    of the product, no task beyond the one complaint, and will not come back to
//    check whether it worked. Everything here therefore takes effect on the spot
//    and shows its result on the spot. WHO IS NOT HERE: the administrator, whose
//    assignments decide this reader's organization and role. Their absence is
//    named on screen rather than mocked up as rows this reader cannot move —
//    see firma2-settings-account.
//
// 3. SERIES VS SCALAR: it is all scalar. Six independent values in three groups,
//    no list, no table, and nothing that passes the rail test — so the page is a
//    single column of cards and has no rail.
//
// 4. WHAT IS IRREVERSIBLE: nothing. Every control here is a value you can set
//    back in one gesture, and none of them touch a record, a report, or money.
//    That is why no control has a confirmation and the page has no danger zone —
//    the brief reserves the room and the explanation for the irreversible, and
//    spending it on a colour scheme would teach readers to click through the
//    warnings that matter.
//
// 5. WHAT WAS CUT: the Save button, for the brief's always-editable /
//    always-saved rule — the scheme has already repainted the page by the time
//    you would reach for it. Also cut: theme preview swatches (the page is the
//    preview), a "Danger zone" (nothing here is dangerous), and a Sign out row —
//    the account menu in the top bar already carries it on every screen, and a
//    second one here would be two affordances for one action.
//
// 6. IF THIS CONFIGURES SOMETHING, WHO MEETS IT LATER: nobody. That is the
//    unusual property of this screen and the reason it can be as light as it is
//    — the only person who lives with these choices is the person making them.
//
// ORDER OF THE THREE CARDS: who you are, then how the app looks, then what it
// sends you. Identity first because it is the thing that makes the rest of the
// page legibly about YOU; appearance second because it is the setting that
// actually works and the one most people arrive for; notifications last because
// it is the longest section and the one you scan rather than read.
//
// The app chrome and the page padding come from AppLayout -> esa-app-shell. Zero
// page CSS.
import AppLayout from '../../layouts/AppLayout.astro';
import Firma2PageHeader from '../../components/shared/firma2-page-header.astro';
import Firma2SettingsAccount from '../../components/settings/firma2-settings-account.astro';
import Firma2SettingsAppearance from '../../components/settings/firma2-settings-appearance.astro';
import Firma2SettingsNotifications from '../../components/settings/firma2-settings-notifications.astro';
---
<!-- manifest:
  layout: stack(2xl)
  sections:
    - page header   -> firma2-page-header
    - account       -> firma2-settings-account
    - appearance    -> firma2-settings-appearance
    - notifications -> firma2-settings-notifications
-->
<AppLayout title="Settings — ProjectFirma 2.0" active="settings">
  <div class="stack" data-gap="2xl">
    {/* No crumbs: this is a top-level destination off the account menu, and a
        single crumb above the title would only repeat the title one line up. */}
    <Firma2PageHeader title="Settings" />
    <Firma2SettingsAccount />
    <Firma2SettingsAppearance />
    <Firma2SettingsNotifications />
  </div>
</AppLayout>
